{
  "beruf": "bau-handwerk",
  "title": "Construction and trades",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "The BTP (bâtiment et travaux publics) is one of the biggest ways into work for newcomers in France, often through an agence d'intérim. This deck has the site and its tools, what you do, the orders you will hear, protective equipment, and the people you work with. On site nearly everyone says tu, and instructions are short; with the client, say vous.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work on a building site in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Manœuvre",
    "Maçon / maçonne",
    "Coffreur / coffreuse",
    "Peintre en bâtiment",
    "Plaquiste",
    "Électricien / électricienne"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Orders on site are short and loud: « Attention en dessous ! », « Doucement ! », « Passe-moi la truelle ! » If you don't understand, ask before you move: « Tu peux répéter ? »",
    "Put on your EPI before you start: helmet, safety shoes, gloves, vest, and a harness when you work high up. Asking is normal: « Où est mon casque ? » Say it at once if something looks dangerous.",
    "With the client, say vous: « Bonjour madame Martin, nous venons pour les travaux. » With the team, tu is usual. The chef de chantier or chef d'équipe gives the orders.",
    "Many newcomers start through an agence d'intérim. Ask for your contract and your fiche de paie, and note your hours. Safety training, medical checks and a licence for some machines (for example a CACES) are usually asked for, but it depends on the site and the job: ask the employer, France Travail or a union. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
